> For example,based on a model as attached plot,when theta is 1,the probability (P)is 0.5,which means ability is midpoint of population.

It does not mean that at all. The midpoint of the population depends on the probability density of the theta distribution and is not related to p(theta).

> I am asking what's the probability(P) in mod when theta is 2.2954?

I still don't know what this means. You can find the probability for a given item, p(y=k | theta = 2.2954), by using the probtrace() function in the package, or you can find the expected values (item/bundle/total), E(y=k | theta = 2.2954), using the expected.test() function. It doesn't make sense to think of the whole test in terms of a single probability since the test itself is the sum of probability functions. Perhaps you're asking for something else, but I don't know what it is you are seeking.

