It depends on the shape of the population distribution. You have a few options here: either assume that the population is some form (like normal, see my previous reply), or you can try to estimate its shape somehow, perhaps by comparing to all the saved factors scores or by estimating the shape of this density directly (e.g., via empirical histogram or davidian curves). It depends on which approach you're willing to tolerate, and how much data you have to estimate these associated parameters/effects.

Phil

